Which is impossible. You can limit the amount of attacks landed
against you with good tactics, but not completely. Just look at
anyone's death statistics: If being killed by any physical attack
at all, as opposed to choking or drowning or some other stupid
death, plays a significant role in those statistics, then better
AC could have helped.

> The exact problem, and especially for newbies, is
> that good AC tempts the inexperienced or unwise
> player to a shift to "combat wombat" mode, at which
> point the monster difficulties rapidly escalate to
> overcome the armor improvement.

Don't do that, then. I learned not to do it, and so can any newbie.
It's _the_ lesson to be learned in NetHack, and it doesn't only
apply to AC. Some find Frostbrand on the floor on DL2 and switch to
berserker mode. If you haven't learned _that_ lesson, you'll _never_
get far in NetHack.

> Using "good AC" skillfully in NetHack is very
> knowledge and experience intensive.

No. AC is a backup defense for when all else fails. As long as you
keep that in mind, "using" it is easy.

>> But a light source needs a certain amount of skill
>> to utilize (and it is still going to be worse than
>> DSM).
>
> Contrariwise, "using" a permanent good light source
> is "turn on and forget" for magic lamps, not so for
> Sunsword.
>
> Making use of the information that good lighting
> provides is certainly an acquired skill, but "good
> intelligence" beats "superior armaments" has been
> part of combat gospel at least since Sun Tsu wrote
> "The Art of War".
>
> There's no way "better armor" outweighs "better
> information". NetHack's AC system makes the best
> possible armor class still subject to attack, but
> "seeing the enemy soon enough to evade the enemy"
> and then indeed evading that enemy removes the
> enemy's chance to attack.

Gathering tactical information is important. But you shouldn't 
rely on your vision for that. Even with a magic lamp, the distance 
covered is insufficient. ESP, telepathy, detect monsters via
potion or spell (at skilled, wonderful!), warning, all those have
to be used to their best effect. If you don't keep an eye on the
monster situation of the whole level via telepathy, you won't notice
your retreat paths being blocked. Your magic lamp won't help you one
bit when you see a pack of soldier ants advancing on you and there's
some critters in the corridor back to the stairs. Using means of long
range monster detection, you would have known about the ants and never
wanderd so far towards them in the first place.

The point is, when you don't know how to use your means of monster
detection to best effect, light sources don't help you much.

